linuxdoom-1.10: LP64 + modern-toolchain fixes for a static x86_64 build
=======================================================================

Applies to a pristine copy of github.com/id-software/doom linuxdoom-1.10
(1997-01-06 release).  Apply with `patch -p1` from the source root.

linuxdoom-1.10 was written for 32-bit ILP32 machines (Linux/386, SGI).  It
does not compile or run on x86_64 as published; every hunk below is required,
either to satisfy a C23-default compiler or to remove a 32-bit assumption that
is silently wrong on LP64.  Nothing here changes game logic or the WAD format.

m_misc.c
  * default_t.defaultvalue was `int` but holds either an int or a char*
    (the DOS sound/mouse path names and the ten chat macros).  Casting a
    string literal to `int` is not a constant expression any more -> "error:
    initializer element is not computable at load time".  Widen the field to
    intptr_t (it mirrors the pointer-sized storage the table writes through).
  * M_LoadDefaults stored a char* through an `int*` -> truncated pointer on
    LP64.  Store through intptr_t* instead.
  (M_SaveDefaults already keys off the magnitude of defaultvalue to tell
   string from integer entries, which only works once the field is wide.)

i_sound.c
  * `extern int errno;` inside myioctl referenced a symbol that musl does not
    export (errno is a macro over __errno_location).  Include <errno.h> so the
    diagnostic compiles against the modern definition.

r_data.c
  * maptexture_t is a *file format* struct: on disk the "columndirectory"
    field is 32 bits, but the source declared it `void**`, so on LP64 the
    struct gained 4 bytes of padding and every patch offset read from
    TEXTURE1/TEXTURE2 was garbage (SIGSEGV in R_InitTextures at startup).
    Declare it `int`, matching the WAD.
  * textures/texturecolumnlump/texturecolumnofs/texturecomposite are pointer
    arrays allocated with a hardcoded `*4`; use sizeof(*p) so they get 8-byte
    slots.  (The remaining *4 allocations in that block really are int arrays.)
  * colormaps 256-byte alignment truncated the pointer to int; use intptr_t.

r_draw.c
  * same int-truncating alignment arithmetic for translationtables.

p_setup.c
  * linebuffer is a line_t** array allocated with `*4`; use sizeof(*linebuffer).

p_saveg.c
  * PADSAVEP() aligned save_p by truncating it to int; use intptr_t so save
    games do not corrupt memory.

info.c / info.h
  * sprnames[] has exactly NUMSPRITES (138) initialisers and no NULL
    terminator, yet R_InitSpriteDefs counts entries with `while (*check != 0)`.
    On ILP32 the zero word that happened to follow the array ended the scan;
    on LP64 at -O2 it does not, numsprites comes out as 139+, and startup
    SIGSEGVs in R_InitSprites.  Add the sentinel (array becomes NUMSPRITES+1)
    so the scan has the terminator it was always written to expect.

Known remaining 32-bit assumptions (not exercised by single-player, left
untouched): d_net.c NetbufferChecksum's `(int)&field` offsets (netgames only)
and d_main.c's -statcopy `(void*)atoi(...)` (deathmatch stats only).

EOF
